data.sparql-vs-the-model-query-engine#SPARQL:
For most application development, you won't write raw SPARQL. The model query engine (accessed via Ad4mModel or perspective.modelQuery()) handles query generation, hydration, filtering, sorting, and pagination automatically:
// These are equivalent:
// Raw SPARQL
const results = await perspective.querySparql(`
  SELECT ?todo WHERE {
    ?stateLink a rdf:Statement ;
               rdf:subject ?todo ;
               rdf:predicate <todo://state> ;
               rdf:object <todo://done> .
  }
`);
// Model query (recommended for application code)
const doneTodos = await Todo.findAll(perspective, {
  where: { state: "todo://done" }
});
Use raw SPARQL when you need:
Custom aggregations (COUNT, SUM, AVG)
Complex multi-hop traversals not expressible via the model query DSL
Analytics and reporting queries
Direct access to link metadata
OPTIONAL patterns and UNION queries
Use the model query engine when you need:
CRUD operations on model instances
Type-safe queries with the fluent builder
Automatic hydration of related models
Real-time subscriptions
Pagination with total count

data.security-note#Security Note:
querySparql() only allows read-only SELECT queries. To modify links, use the standard AD4M methods:
// Adding links
await perspective.add({ source: "user://alice", predicate: "follows", target: "user://bob" });
// Removing links
await perspective.remove({ source: "user://alice", predicate: "follows", target: "user://bob" });

data.the-models#The models:
A flow points at data. The data are ordinary model classes.
@Model({ name: "HandoverTask" })
class Task extends Ad4mModel {
  @Property({ through: "handover://task_title", required: true })
  title: string = "";
}
/** A role as plain data. `fromRole` is a ModelQuery over exactly this. */
@Model({ name: "HandoverReviewerRole" })
class ReviewerRole extends Ad4mModel {
  @Property({ through: "handover://role_agent", required: true })
  agent: string = "";
  @Property({ through: "handover://role_domain", required: true })
  domain: string = "";
}
/** What the grant flow's `Granted` state requires — an on-graph endorsement. */
@Model({ name: "HandoverGrantEndorsement" })
class GrantEndorsement extends Ad4mModel {
  @Property({ through: "handover://endorsement_reason", required: true })
  reason: string = "";
}
That block is trimmed. Three more classes carry the task's evidence and are shaped the same way: WorkLog (HandoverWorkLog, one note), Deliverable (HandoverDeliverable, one title) and ReviewNote (HandoverReviewNote, one body). All six are registered on the perspective before any flow runs.Two things to notice:
A role is a class, not a special object. ReviewerRole has an agent property that holds a DID, and a domain. The gate will query it like any other class.
The endorsement is the grant flow's guard evidence. Granted requires one GrantEndorsement linked to the role instance. Without it nobody can even propose the grant.
Evidence is created under its subject with a parent link. The test wraps that in a helper, with HAS_CHILD set to "ad4m://has_child":
async function createUnder<T extends Ad4mModel>(
  model: any,
  p: PerspectiveProxy,
  parentUri: string,
  data: Record<string, any>,
): Promise<T> {
  return (await model.create(p, data, {
    parent: { id: parentUri, predicate: HAS_CHILD },
  })) as T;
}

data.step-1-the-task-flow#Step 1: the task fl:
makeTaskFlow builds the four-state flow. It takes the Done state's consensus rule as a parameter, because every step below changes only that rule.
function makeTaskFlow(doneRule: ConsensusRule, name = "TaskFlow"): SHACLFlow {
  const flow = new SHACLFlow(name, "handover://");
  flow.inputTypes = ["HandoverTask"];
  flow.consensusRule = { n: 1 };
  flow.interpretationHint =
    "Advance a Task: Ready → InProgress → InReview → Done.";
  flow.addState({
    name: "Ready",
    value: 0,
    interpretationHint: "Named, not yet started.",
  });
  flow.addState({
    name: "InProgress",
    value: 1,
    interpretationHint: "Someone is working on it.",
    requires: [{ className: "HandoverWorkLog", linkedTo: "base", count: { min: 1 } }],
  });
  flow.addState({
    name: "InReview",
    value: 2,
    interpretationHint: "A deliverable exists and is waiting on review.",
    requires: [{ className: "HandoverDeliverable", linkedTo: "base", count: { min: 1 } }],
  });
  flow.addState({
    name: "Done",
    value: 3,
    interpretationHint: "Accepted.",
    requires: [
      { className: "HandoverDeliverable", linkedTo: "base", count: { min: 1 } },
      { className: "HandoverReviewNote", linkedTo: "base", count: { min: 1 } },
    ],
    consensusRule: doneRule,
  });
  flow.addTransition({ actionName: "Start", fromState: "Ready", toState: "InProgress", actions: [] });
  flow.addTransition({ actionName: "SubmitForReview", fromState: "InProgress", toState: "InReview", actions: [] });
  flow.addTransition({ actionName: "Accept", fromState: "InReview", toState: "Done", actions: [] });
  return flow;
}
The name parameter matters later. A flow's identity is ${namespace}${name}Flow, so makeTaskFlow(rule) is handover://TaskFlowFlow and makeTaskFlow(rule, "GatedTaskFlow") is handover://GatedTaskFlowFlow. Two definitions with the same name on one perspective would collide.Part 1 of the test drives this flow with doneRule = { n: 2 } and no role. Register it, create a task, start a run:
await aliceP.addFlow("TaskFlow", makeTaskFlow({ n: 2 }));
const task = (await (Task as any).create(aliceP, { title: "Ship the handover" })) as Task;
const started = await FlowInstance.start(aliceP, "TaskFlow", task.id);
expect(started.currentStateName, "lowest-valued state is where every run begins").to.equal("Ready");
The run reaches InReview through a helper the later steps reuse. Bob writes a work log and presses Start, then writes a deliverable and presses SubmitForReview. Both edges are n: 1, so each press fires:
async function advanceToInReview(
  aliceP: PerspectiveProxy,
  bobP: PerspectiveProxy,
  taskId: string,
): Promise<void> {
  await createUnder<WorkLog>(WorkLog, bobP, taskId, { note: "Picked up." });
  const started = await (await instanceOn(bobP, taskId)).proposeTransition(
    "InProgress",
    "I've started — see the log",
  );
  expect(started.outcomes, "n:1 Start fires on proposeTransition").to.have.lengthOf(1);
  expect(started.recordedVote).to.be.true;
  expect(started.derivedState).to.equal("InProgress");
  await createUnder<Deliverable>(Deliverable, bobP, taskId, { title: "patch.ts" });
  const submitted = await (await instanceOn(bobP, taskId)).proposeTransition("InReview");
  expect(submitted.outcomes).to.have.lengthOf(1);
  expect(submitted.derivedState).to.equal("InReview");
  const aliceView = await instanceOn(aliceP, taskId);
  expect(aliceView.currentStateName).to.equal("InReview");
}
instanceOn(p, subject) is FlowInstance.findAll(p, { subject }) with an assertion that exactly one run exists. The test re-fetches the instance before every press because FlowInstance is a snapshot, not a live object.With { n: 2 } on Done, Alice's press records a vote and fires nothing. Bob presses the same button and co-signs her proposal; two distinct DIDs is quorum and the edge fires. Part 1 then shows that Bob withdrawing his vote moves the run back to InReview. The Flows page covers that in full. What this guide needs from Part 1 is only the shape: press, count, fire.

data.step-2-gate-done-on-a-role#Step 2: gate:
Part 2 replaces { n: 2 } with a rule that counts one vote, but only from a DID that holds a ReviewerRole for the frontend domain:
await aliceP.addFlow(
  "TaskFlow",
  makeTaskFlow({
    n: 1,
    fromRole: {
      className: "HandoverReviewerRole",
      where: { domain: "frontend" },
      didProperty: "agent",
    },
  }),
);
fromRole is a model query. didProperty names the property whose value is the voter's DID. For each voter the engine asks: is there a HandoverReviewerRole with domain = "frontend" and agent = <this DID>?Bob gets a role instance. Alice does not:
const role = await createUnder<ReviewerRole>(ReviewerRole, aliceP, task.id, {
  agent: bobDid,
  domain: "frontend",
});
Alice presses Accept first. Her vote lands, but the derivation does not count it, so the n: 1 edge does not fire. Bob presses next and the edge fires with Bob alone as the voter:
const alicePress = await (await instanceOn(aliceP, task.id)).proposeTransition("Done");
expect(
  alicePress.outcomes,
  "a non-reviewer click must not settle Done — if this fires, fromRole is being ignored",
).to.have.lengthOf(0);
expect(alicePress.recordedVote, "the click still wrote a vote; it just does not count").to.be.true;
expect((await instanceOn(aliceP, task.id)).currentStateName).to.equal("InReview");
const bobPress = await (await instanceOn(bobP, task.id)).proposeTransition("Done");
expect(bobPress.outcomes, "the reviewer DID is the one fromRole counts").to.have.lengthOf(1);
expect(bobPress.outcomes[0].toState).to.equal("Done");
expect(bobPress.outcomes[0].voters).to.include(bobDid);
expect(bobPress.outcomes[0].voters).to.not.include(aliceDid);
Notice what made Bob a reviewer: Alice created a link. Nothing else. Any member who can write a ReviewerRole instance can make anyone a reviewer. Part 3 turns that into a process.

data.step-3-the-grant-flow#Step 3: the grant:
A role grant is itself a flow. It runs over the role instance as its subject:
function makeGrantFlow(): SHACLFlow {
  const flow = new SHACLFlow("ReviewerRoleGrant", "handover://");
  flow.inputTypes = ["HandoverReviewerRole"];
  flow.consensusRule = { n: 1 };
  flow.interpretationHint =
    "Grant a ReviewerRole: Proposed → Granted. Completing this flow is what producedByFlow binds to.";
  flow.addState({
    name: "Proposed",
    value: 0,
    interpretationHint: "This agent has been nominated as a reviewer.",
  });
  flow.addState({
    name: "Granted",
    value: 1,
    interpretationHint: "The group agreed this agent holds the role.",
    requires: [{ className: "HandoverGrantEndorsement", linkedTo: "base", count: { min: 1 } }],
    consensusRule: { n: 2 },
  });
  flow.addTransition({
    actionName: "Grant",
    fromState: "Proposed",
    toState: "Granted",
    actions: [],
  });
  return flow;
}
Its URI is handover://ReviewerRoleGrantFlow. The test keeps that in a constant, GRANT_FLOW_URI, because the gate in Step 6 has to name it exactly.Nominating is creating the role instance. The instance is the grant flow's base expression. Creating it says "Bob is proposed as a frontend reviewer". Starting the grant flow over it opens the group's decision:
await aliceP.addFlow("ReviewerRoleGrant", makeGrantFlow());
const role = (await (ReviewerRole as any).create(aliceP, {
  agent: bobDid,
  domain: "frontend",
})) as ReviewerRole;
const grant = await FlowInstance.start(aliceP, "ReviewerRoleGrant", role.id);
expect(grant.currentStateName).to.equal("Proposed");
expect(grant.availableTransitions.map((t) => t.actionName)).to.deep.equal(["Grant"]);
expect(grant.subject).to.equal(role.id);
At this point Bob's role instance exists and the grant sits in Proposed. Nobody has agreed to anything yet.

data.step-4-the-hole#Step 4: the hole:
Now register the Part 2 task flow, with plain fromRole, next to the grant flow. Create a task, advance it to InReview, add a review note. Then, with the grant still in Proposed, Bob presses Accept:
expect((await instanceOn(aliceP, role.id)).currentStateName, "grant still Proposed").to.equal(
  "Proposed",
);
const openDone = await (await instanceOn(bobP, openTask.id)).proposeTransition("Done");
expect(
  openDone.outcomes,
  "HOLE: fromRole matched the ReviewerRole instance while its grant flow was still Proposed",
).to.have.lengthOf(1);
expect(openDone.derivedState).to.equal("Done");
It fires. Plain fromRole asks whether a matching instance exists. It does. The grant flow running over that instance is invisible to the query. The group has a nomination process and the gate ignores it.This is a hole, not a bug. fromRole does exactly what it says: match an instance. The test asserts the behaviour so that nobody mistakes it for a guarantee. To bind the gate to the process, the role query needs one more condition.

data.step-5-complete-the-grant-naming-its-out:
The grant flow's Granted state is n: 2, so Alice and Bob both press Grant. First the guard evidence:
const grantOutputs = [{ className: "HandoverReviewerRole", id: role.id }];
await createUnder<GrantEndorsement>(GrantEndorsement, aliceP, role.id, {
  reason: "Bob reviewed the last three frontend tasks.",
});
Then two presses, each naming the role instance as the run's output:
const aliceGrant = await (await instanceOn(aliceP, role.id)).proposeTransition(
  "Granted",
  undefined,
  grantOutputs,
);
expect(aliceGrant.outcomes, "grant is n:2 — Alice alone does not grant").to.have.lengthOf(0);
expect(aliceGrant.recordedVote).to.be.true;
expect(aliceGrant.derivedState).to.equal("Proposed");
const bobGrant = await (await instanceOn(bobP, role.id)).proposeTransition(
  "Granted",
  undefined,
  grantOutputs,
);
expect(bobGrant.outcomes, "two DIDs settle the grant flow").to.have.lengthOf(1);
expect(bobGrant.minted, "Bob co-signed Alice's proposal, outputs and all").to.be.false;
expect(bobGrant.derivedState).to.equal("Granted");
expect((await instanceOn(aliceP, role.id)).currentStateName).to.equal("Granted");
const grantRunUri = bobGrant.outcomes[0].instanceUri;
The third argument to proposeTransition is the list of outputs. Three rules apply, each enforced by the executor:
The subject is never an output by default. The run's subject is the role instance, but a receipt speaks only for what the quorum named. Leave outputs empty and the receipt grants nothing.
Outputs are only accepted on a proposal into a terminal state. Granted has no outgoing edge, so the run ends there. Naming outputs on a non-terminal edge is refused.
Every co-signer must name the same outputs. The proposal signs a hash over each output's class, id and content. Bob's press with the same grantOutputs joins Alice's proposal, which is why minted is false. A press naming different outputs is refused rather than joined: joining would sign something the presser did not name.
Why the content is in the hash, and not just the id, is explained under Outputs.Keep grantRunUri. It is the completed run, and the next step mints its receipt. Note that completing the grant did not close the hole from Step 4. The plain fromRole gate still matches the instance, exactly as before.

data.step-6-producedbyflow-fail-closed-before:
Register a second task flow under a different name, whose Done rule adds producedByFlow to the role query:
await aliceP.addFlow(
  "GatedTaskFlow",
  makeTaskFlow(
    {
      n: 1,
      fromRole: {
        className: "HandoverReviewerRole",
        where: { domain: "frontend" },
        didProperty: "agent",
        producedByFlow: { flow: GRANT_FLOW_URI, state: "Granted" },
      },
    },
    "GatedTaskFlow",
  ),
);
producedByFlow adds one condition: the matched role instance must be a verified output of a run of handover://ReviewerRoleGrantFlow that settled into Granted. Both flow and state are required on a role. Without state, a run that settled into a rejecting terminal state would grant too, so the rule refuses to decode instead.Create a fresh task on GatedTaskFlow, advance it, add a review note, and let Bob press Accept. His grant run has settled. Nobody has minted its receipt:
const gatedDone = await (await instanceOn(bobP, gatedTask.id)).proposeTransition("Done");
expect(
  gatedDone.outcomes,
  "producedByFlow without a receipt must not count Bob — if this fires, the field is being ignored",
).to.have.lengthOf(0);
expect(gatedDone.recordedVote, "the click still wrote; the fold did not count it").to.be.true;
expect(gatedDone.derivedState).to.equal("InReview");
expect((await instanceOn(aliceP, gatedTask.id)).currentStateName).to.equal("InReview");
expect(
  await aliceP.flowValidOutputs(GRANT_FLOW_URI, "Granted"),
  "no receipt yet, so the grant flow has no valid outputs",
).to.be.empty;
Bob does not count. The run completed is not evidence. A receipt is. The gate reads receipts from the granting flow's index and verifies each one; with no receipt there is nothing to verify, and an unverified grant is not a grant. flowValidOutputs asks the same question from the outside and gives the same empty answer.
Spell producedByFlow exactly. A misspelt key, or the pre-rename
grantedByFlow, makes the whole rule unreadable, and addFlow refuses the
definition up front with an error naming the state and the key; nothing is
stored. A definition that arrived by sync with such a rule is refused when
it is read: the edge is refused for everyone, Bob included, and the
executor log names the key
(#1144). It never falls
back to plain fromRole.

data.step-7-mint-the-receipt#Step 7: mint the:
Any member mints once the run has settled. Nothing mints automatically.
const minted = await aliceP.mintFlowReceipt(grantRunUri);
expect(minted.receiptUri).to.match(/^ad4m:\/\/flow\/receipt\//);
The mint re-derives the grant run's state, collects the outputs the final edge's quorum named, checks their hash against what the quorum signed, and writes the receipt plus its discovery links:
  receipt ──ad4m://flow/receipt_content──▶ (the receipt body, as a literal)
  run     ──ad4m://flow/receipt──────────▶ receipt
  flow    ──ad4m://flow/flow_receipt─────▶ receipt   ◀── the gate reads this index
  output  ──ad4m://flow/granted_by───────▶ receipt   (one per named output)
The third link is the one that matters here. handover://ReviewerRoleGrantFlow now has one entry in its receipt index. When the gate evaluates producedByFlow it loads every receipt under that index, verifies each against this replica's own copy of the flow definition, and keeps the ones that were minted for this flow, settled into Granted, and name the instance as (HandoverReviewerRole, role.id).Ask the same question through the SDK:
const granted = await aliceP.flowValidOutputs(GRANT_FLOW_URI, "Granted");
expect(granted).to.have.lengthOf(1);
expect(granted[0].output).to.deep.equal({ className: "HandoverReviewerRole", id: role.id });
expect(granted[0].receiptUri).to.equal(minted.receiptUri);
const verdict = await bobP.verifyFlowReceipt(minted.receipt);
expect(verdict.outcome, verdict.detail).to.equal("verified");
expect(verdict.voters).to.have.members([aliceDid, bobDid]);
Now the gated task from Step 6. Bob's vote is still on the graph, unchanged. The grant is dated from the receipt's settled_at, the moment Bob's Grant press reached quorum, and Bob voted on the gated task after that. So the same vote counts now, and the run derives Done with nothing new written.There is no read that re-derives without voting, so the test probes with the button. The refusal names the derived state:
let reDerived = "";
try {
  await (await instanceOn(bobP, gatedTask.id)).proposeTransition("Done");
} catch (e: any) {
  reDerived = String(e?.message ?? e);
}
expect(
  reDerived,
  "the vote Bob cast before the mint counts once the receipt exists — the fold is already in Done",
).to.match(/`Done` is not reachable from `Done`/);
That is the one rule in action. State is derived from the links present now. The links now include a receipt, so the derivation changed. Nobody re-voted.

data.step-8-a-holder-and-a-nominee#Step 8: a:
The last part sharpens the negative case. Alice creates her own ReviewerRole instance, same class, same domain, and nominates herself through the same grant flow. Her run stays in Proposed, so there is nothing to mint:
const aliceRole = (await (ReviewerRole as any).create(aliceP, {
  agent: aliceDid,
  domain: "frontend",
})) as ReviewerRole;
const aliceNomination = await FlowInstance.start(aliceP, "ReviewerRoleGrant", aliceRole.id);
expect(aliceNomination.currentStateName).to.equal("Proposed");
let unminted = "";
try {
  await aliceP.mintFlowReceipt(aliceNomination.uri);
} catch (e: any) {
  unminted = String(e?.message ?? e);
}
expect(unminted, "a run that has not settled has nothing to mint").to.match(/no settled edge/);
On a fresh GatedTaskFlow task, Alice presses first. She matches the where-clause through her own instance. Only the receipt binding keeps her out, because Bob's receipt names Bob's instance, not hers:
const aliceDone = await (await instanceOn(aliceP, positiveTask.id)).proposeTransition("Done");
expect(
  aliceDone.outcomes,
  "Alice's instance was never granted — if this fires, the receipt is not bound to its instance",
).to.have.lengthOf(0);
expect(aliceDone.recordedVote).to.be.true;
expect(aliceDone.derivedState).to.equal("InReview");
const bobDone = await (await instanceOn(bobP, positiveTask.id)).proposeTransition("Done");
expect(
  bobDone.outcomes,
  "Bob's role instance is a verified output of a completed grant run — he counts",
).to.have.lengthOf(1);
expect(bobDone.outcomes[0].toState).to.equal("Done");
expect(bobDone.outcomes[0].voters).to.include(bobDid);
expect(bobDone.outcomes[0].voters, "Alice's earlier click still does not count").to.not.include(
  aliceDid,
);
A receipt never says "flow F completed". It says "flow F produced this (className, id)". That is why Alice's instance, identical in every property the query looks at, does not pass.

data.anatomy-of-an-expression#Anatomy of an E:
Every Expression consists of:
interface Expression {
    // The DID of the agent who created this Expression
    author: string;

    // When the Expression was created
    timestamp: string;

    // The actual data/content
    data: any;

    // Which Language this Expression belongs to
    language: LanguageRef;

    // Cryptographic proof of authorship
    proof: {
        // These flags are added by AD4M during retrieval
        valid?: boolean;    // True if signature verification succeeded
        invalid?: boolean;  // True if signature verification failed
        // The actual stored proof data
        signature: string;  // The cryptographic signature
        key: string;       // Key path in the author's DID Document used for signing
    };
}
For example:
{
  "author": "did:key:zQ3shv5VUAbtY5P1jGsPzy7L27FTxeDv2hnhVp9QT3ALNCnQ2",
  "timestamp": "2023-06-21T14:47:48.935Z",
  "data": "Hello World!",
  "language": {
    "address": "literal"
  },
  "proof": {
    "valid": true,     // Added by AD4M during retrieval
    "invalid": false,  // Added by AD4M during retrieval
    "signature": "...", // The stored signature
    "key": "..." // Key path in the author's DID Document used for signing
  }
}

data.creating-expressions#Creating Expression:
When an agent wants to share data, they create an Expression through a Language:
// Create an Expression using the literal Language
const url = await ad4m.expression.create(
    "Hello World!", 
    "literal"
);
// Returns: literal:base64encoded-content
// Create an Expression using a custom Language
const socialPost = await ad4m.expression.create(
    { text: "My first post!" },
    "QmSocialApp123" // Language hash
);
// Returns: QmSocialApp123://post789
Behind the scenes, AD4M:
Passes the data to the specified Language
The Language stores/processes the data and returns an address
AD4M signs the Expression with the agent's keys
Returns the full Expression URL

data.signature-verification#Signature Verific:
When you retrieve an Expression through ad4m.expression.get(), AD4M automatically:
Resolves the author's DID to get their public keys
Verifies that the signature matches:
The author's DID
The Expression's data
The timestamp
Using the specified key from the author's DID Document
Enriches the Expression object with verification flags:
proof.valid: Set to true if verification succeeded
proof.invalid: Set to true if verification failed
These verification flags are not stored with the Expression – they are computed on-the-fly during retrieval to ensure fresh verification against the current state of the author's DID Document.
// When you retrieve an Expression
const expression = await ad4m.expression.get("QmSocialApp123://post789");
// AD4M has already verified the signature
if (expression.proof.valid) {
    // The Expression is authentic and unmodified
    console.log("Verified expression from:", expression.author);
} else {
    // The signature verification failed
    console.warn("Could not verify expression authenticity");
}

data:
A flow is a shared state machine. A group moves it forward with signed proposals and votes. The flow runs over an expression that lives in a shared Perspective, and every replica can recompute its state from links it can verify.That is the whole difference from a status field. A status field is whatever the last writer said. A flow's state is what the group's own rules produced: every transition is backed by an on-graph proposal, gated by a consensus rule that says how many distinct agents must sign before it counts.With flows you can build, among other things:
a task handover — Ready → InProgress → InReview → Done, where Done needs a deliverable, a review note, and a reviewer's signature;
a review process — a state that requires evidence in the graph before anyone can even propose entering it;
a role grant — a flow whose completion, proven by a receipt, is what makes someone a reviewer in another flow.
Flow definitions are Social DNA: a flow is data in the Perspective, shared like any other link. Two agents in a Neighbourhood run the same flow because they hold the same definition, not because they run the same app.

data.states-and-transitions#States and transi:
A flow is defined in TypeScript with SHACLFlow, then registered on a perspective with addFlow. The example below is the task flow the handover test drives end to end.
import { SHACLFlow } from "@coasys/ad4m";
const flow = new SHACLFlow("TaskFlow", "handover://");
flow.inputTypes = ["HandoverTask"];      // subject classes this flow runs over
flow.consensusRule = { n: 1 };           // default rule for every transition
flow.interpretationHint =
  "Advance a Task: Ready → InProgress → InReview → Done.";
flow.addState({
  name: "Ready",
  value: 0,                              // lowest value = the initial state
  interpretationHint: "Named, not yet started.",
});
flow.addState({
  name: "InProgress",
  value: 1,
  interpretationHint: "Someone is working on it.",
  requires: [{ className: "HandoverWorkLog", linkedTo: "base", count: { min: 1 } }],
});
flow.addState({
  name: "InReview",
  value: 2,
  interpretationHint: "A deliverable exists and is waiting on review.",
  requires: [{ className: "HandoverDeliverable", linkedTo: "base", count: { min: 1 } }],
});
flow.addState({
  name: "Done",
  value: 3,
  interpretationHint: "Accepted.",
  requires: [
    { className: "HandoverDeliverable", linkedTo: "base", count: { min: 1 } },
    { className: "HandoverReviewNote", linkedTo: "base", count: { min: 1 } },
  ],
  consensusRule: { n: 2 },               // per-state override of the default
});
flow.addTransition({ actionName: "Start", fromState: "Ready", toState: "InProgress", actions: [] });
flow.addTransition({ actionName: "SubmitForReview", fromState: "InProgress", toState: "InReview", actions: [] });
flow.addTransition({ actionName: "Accept", fromState: "InReview", toState: "Done", actions: [] });
await perspective.addFlow("TaskFlow", flow);
From tests/js/tests/model/flow-task-handover.test.ts, makeTaskFlow, driven by "Part 1 — Ready → InProgress → InReview → Done via proposeTransition; reject moves it back".Facts about this shape:
value is an ordering key, not a progress percentage. 0, 1, 2 and 0, 10, 20 behave the same. Every run begins in the lowest-valued state.
The flow's canonical identity is its flowUri: ${namespace}${name}Flow, here handover://TaskFlowFlow. Instances store that URI, because bare names collide when two communities share Social DNA.
Each state's interpretationHint is one English sentence. It is rendered into the prompt an interpreting LLM sees for that state, so an agent reasoning about a transition reads what the group meant, not a guess from the name.
A per-state consensusRule overrides the flow-level default. Cheap early edges can take one signature while the final one takes two, or a reviewer.
FlowTransition.actions exists on the type and nothing executes it yet.
Starting a run and rendering it:
import { FlowInstance } from "@coasys/ad4m";
const task = await Task.create(perspective, { title: "Ship the handover" });
const started = await FlowInstance.start(perspective, "TaskFlow", task.id);
started.currentStateName;                              // "Ready"
started.availableTransitions.map((t) => t.actionName); // ["Start"] — the button list
From flow-task-handover.test.ts, Part 1.availableTransitions lists the edges that leave the current state. Render one button per entry. An empty list means the state is terminal.To find runs later, use FlowInstance.findAll(perspective, filter?). The filter is a flow name or { flowName?, subject? }. findAll never registers classes: on a perspective that has never minted an instance it returns [] instead of installing the shape, because a read must not sync a write to every peer.

data.guards-requires#Guards: requires:
requires is where "Done" stops meaning somebody clicked Done and starts meaning the graph contains what Done requires. Each entry is a model query: a subject class, optional property conditions, a cardinality, and an anchor (linkedTo: "base" ties the match to the flow's subject). All entries in the array must match.A proposal into a guarded state is refused while the guard is unmet:
// InProgress requires a HandoverWorkLog linked to the task. None exists yet:
try {
  await started.proposeTransition("InProgress");
} catch (e) {
  // error message names the unmet guard
}
// Write the evidence, then the same press succeeds:
await WorkLog.create(perspective, { note: "Picked up." },
  { parent: { id: task.id, predicate: "ad4m://has_child" } });
const result = await started.proposeTransition("InProgress", "I've started — see the log");
result.outcomes;      // [{ fromState: "Ready", toState: "InProgress", ... }] — n:1 fires
result.derivedState;  // "InProgress"
From flow-task-handover.test.ts, Part 1 and advanceToInReview.When a proposal is minted, the executor evaluates the guard on the proposing replica and seals the evidence: it writes the matched instances as the proposal's evidence and an order-independent SHA-256 over their canonicalised content next to them. Every voter re-computes that seal against their own graph before they sign. If the cited content changed since the mint, the voter's replica declines to co-sign and writes nothing. It does not delete the proposal — the local graph may simply be behind, and a replica may only ever refuse its own action.The derivation itself re-runs no guards. Every vote it counts was cast by someone who checked the seal first, and settled history is never re-tested against the live graph. Otherwise editing a task that a finished transition had cited would silently unwind the flow that consumed it.
The engine also runs a deterministic evaluator after each LLM extraction
pass. It checks every live instance against the requires guards of the
reachable next states and mints a proposal for each fully satisfied pair.
That keeps a flow moving when an LLM produced the evidence but did not
press the button. A state with no guard is never proposed this way —
nothing in the graph implies a human started work — so guard-free states
advance only through proposeTransition. An LLM's own proposal never fires
a transition on its own.

data.reject-is-not-cancel#Reject is not cance:
rejectProposal withdraws what you signed. It deletes only links this DID signed — your vote, or your whole proposal if you opened it — and returns how many links went. It never touches another agent's links. If you signed nothing on the proposal it errors instead of doing nothing silently.Because state is recomputed from the links present now, withdrawing a vote that had settled an edge moves the flow back:
// Done had just settled at n:2. Bob withdraws his vote:
const retracted = await bobInstance.rejectProposal(bobPress.proposalUri); // ≥ 1 link removed
// The next derivation stands in InReview again — Alice's single vote
// is one short of n:2:
const after = await aliceInstance.proposeTransition("Done");
after.derivedState;  // "InReview"
after.outcomes;      // [] — Alice already voted
From flow-task-handover.test.ts, Part 1.There is no "already fired" guard, and there must not be one. The resolved_as → "fired" mark the engine writes is a Local display index, never an input to the derivation.

data.roles-fromrole-and-didproperty#Roles: fr:
A ConsensusRule can require that voters hold a role. A role is not a special object. It is ordinary data, and fromRole is an ordinary model query over it:
flow.addState({
  name: "Done",
  value: 3,
  requires: [ /* … as above … */ ],
  consensusRule: {
    n: 1,
    fromRole: {
      className: "HandoverReviewerRole",
      where: { domain: "frontend" },
      didProperty: "agent",   // the property on each match that holds the member's DID
    },
  },
});
A non-member can still press the button. Their vote is recorded, but the derivation does not count it:
// Bob holds a ReviewerRole instance with agent = bobDid. Alice does not.
const alicePress = await aliceInstance.proposeTransition("Done");
alicePress.outcomes;      // [] — her click does not settle an n:1 edge
alicePress.recordedVote;  // true — the vote landed; it just does not count
const bobPress = await bobInstance.proposeTransition("Done");
bobPress.outcomes[0].voters;  // includes bobDid, not aliceDid
bobPress.derivedState;        // "Done"
From flow-task-handover.test.ts, "Part 2 — Done gated by fromRole: only the reviewer DID counts".Each vote is gated as of its own timestamp. A voter who held the role when they signed still counts after the role is revoked, and a voter who signed before the grant does not count retroactively. Revocation is a signed tombstone link on the role instance, never a deletion. It stops future votes and does not rewrite settled history.One convention follows for Social DNA authors: role instances are add-only, and membership ends only through tombstones. The role query runs against the current graph — only the instance's existence is windowed in time — so a query keyed on a mutable property (where: { active: true }) reopens the deletion problem: flipping the property would remove the instance from historical verdicts and un-settle edges its votes once settled. State that changes belongs in new instances, not in edited ones.
Plain fromRole matches the role instance. Anyone who can write a
matching instance passes the gate. Part 3 of the handover test asserts this
as "the hole": a ReviewerRoleGrant flow over the role instance was still
in Proposed, and fromRole matched the instance anyway. To bind
membership to a grant process completing, add
producedByFlow to the role query.

data.receipts-mintflowreceipt-and-verifyflowr:
A settled run leaves no portable proof by itself. A flow receipt is that proof, and minting one is an explicit call:
// Any member can mint once the run has settled. Nothing mints automatically.
const minted = await perspective.mintFlowReceipt(instanceUri);
minted.receiptUri;  // "ad4m://flow/receipt/…"
minted.receipt;     // the receipt object — hand it to any replica
// Any holder of the receipt can verify it against their own flow catalogue:
const verdict = await perspective.verifyFlowReceipt(minted.receipt);
verdict.outcome;        // "verified" | "rejected" | "undecidable"
verdict.terminalState;  // "Done"
verdict.outputs;        // [{ className, id }] — what the quorum committed to
verdict.voters;         // the distinct DIDs that settled the final edge
From tests/js/tests/flow/flow-produced-by.test.ts, "complete → mint → enumerate → verify → filter, through the SDK", and flow-task-handover.test.ts, Part 3.What a verified receipt proves. Under the flow definition the verifier holds: n distinct eligible DIDs each signed a vote naming a proposal URI that is the content address of this evidence seal and this outputs commitment, the carried read-set re-folds to the claimed terminal state, and the receipt's outputs hash to the commitment that quorum signed. Nothing in the receipt is a minter's assertion of state. The mint itself re-derives the state and refuses to build a receipt for any other.What a receipt does not prove — see Guarantees for the full list. The two that matter most in practice: it does not prove any voter actually re-checked the seal before signing (an honest client does, but the check itself is not signed), and it does not prove the outputs still have the committed content now. A receipt attests to content at completion. A later edit does not invalidate a minted receipt — its preimages are frozen inside it.Verification never re-runs guards and never queries the live graph. The receipt carries the signed material, and the verdict comes from re-folding that material under the verifier's own catalogue. A receipt minted under an older flow definition stops verifying when the definition changes — the verdict names that reason (DnaChanged) rather than calling the receipt bad.The three-way verdict is deliberate. undecidable means "this replica cannot decide", not "the receipt is bad". Do not collapse it into rejected in a UI, and never treat it as verified.Tampering is caught: the produced-by test copies a real receipt, swaps one output id to a node the quorum never committed to, and gets rejected.

data.producedbyflow-querying-what-a-flow-prod:
Every consumer of receipts asks the same question: is this instance a valid output of flow F, settled into state S? The engine answers it once, fail-closed, on three surfaces.Enumerate — perspective.flowValidOutputs(flowUri, state?):
const outputs = await perspective.flowValidOutputs("handover://ProducedDeliveryFlow", "Done");
outputs[0].output;        // { className: "ProducedDeliverable", id: … }
outputs[0].terminalState; // "Done"
outputs[0].receiptUri;    // the receipt that proves it
Filter — the model-query where: { producedByFlow }:
// Only instances a verifying receipt commits to pass the filter:
const produced = await Deliverable.findAll(perspective, {
  where: { producedByFlow: { flow: FLOW_URI, state: "Done" } },
});
// The run's subject is not an output, so it does not pass:
const tasksProduced = await Task.findAll(perspective, {
  where: { producedByFlow: { flow: FLOW_URI } },
});
// tasksProduced === []
Both from flow-produced-by.test.ts, "complete → mint → enumerate → verify → filter, through the SDK".An instance counts iff some receipt in the perspective verifies under this replica's catalogue, was minted for that flow, settled into the named state (any terminal state when omitted), and names the instance by className and id together in its committed outputs. The className is the one in your query — a node committed as a Task is not a produced Role, even with the same id.These two live surfaces apply one more check the receipt alone cannot: the instance's live content must still equal the committed content. An output edited after completion drops from flowValidOutputs and from the filter, while verifyFlowReceipt keeps saying verified — the receipt is about completion time, the query is about now.Fail-closed rules on every surface: a rejected receipt is excluded, an undecidable receipt is excluded the same way (a payout query must not trust a receipt nobody could check), an unknown flow is an error rather than an empty list, and a malformed filter is an error rather than a filter that admits everything.

data:
@coasys/ad4m / Exports / Ad4mModelBase class for defining data models in AD4M.DescriptionAd4mModel provides the foundation for creating data models that are stored in AD4M perspectives.
Each model instance is represented as a subgraph in the perspective, with properties and relations
mapped to links in that graph. The class uses Prolog-based queries to efficiently search and filter
instances based on their properties and relationships.Key concepts:
Each model instance has a unique base expression that serves as its identifier
Properties are stored as links with predicates defined by the through option
Relations represent one-to-many relationships as sets of links
Queries are translated to Prolog for efficient graph pattern matching
Changes are tracked through the perspective's subscription system
Example
// Define a recipe model
@Model({ name: "Recipe" })
class Recipe extends Ad4mModel {
  // Plain property — no `resolveLanguage` → deterministic typed literal
  // storage (fast POS-index path). This is the perf default.
  @Property({ through: "recipe://name" })
  name: string = "";
  // Property that needs per-value provenance (e.g. a signed message body):
  // `resolveLanguage: "literal"` routes values through expression_create on
  // the built-in literal language, producing a signed envelope URI
  // (author/timestamp/proof) instead of a bare typed literal.
  @Property({
    through: "recipe://signed_note",
    resolveLanguage: "literal"
  })
  signedNote: string = "";
  // Property resolved through a custom language: values are routed through
  // expression_create on that language (signed expression URIs).
  @Optional({
    through: "recipe://photo",
    resolveLanguage: "QmFileStorageLanguageAddress..."
  })
  photo: string = "";
  // Optional property with custom initial value
  @Optional({
    through: "recipe://status",
    initial: "recipe://draft"
  })
  status: string = "";
  // Read-only computed property
  @ReadOnly({
    through: "recipe://rating",
    getter: `
      findall(Rating, triple(Base, "recipe://user_rating", Rating), Ratings),
      sum_list(Ratings, Sum),
      length(Ratings, Count),
      Value is Sum / Count
    `
  })
  averageRating: number = 0;
  // Relation of ingredients
  *   @HasMany({ through: "recipe://ingredient" })
  *   ingredients: string[] = [];
  // Relation of comments linked to another model
  @HasMany(() => Comment, { through: "recipe://comment" })
  comments: Comment[] = [];
}
// Create and save a new recipe
const recipe = new Recipe(perspective);
recipe.name = "Chocolate Cake";
recipe.ingredients = ["flour", "sugar", "cocoa"];
await recipe.save();
// Query recipes in different ways
// Get all recipes
const allRecipes = await Recipe.findAll(perspective);
// Find recipes with specific criteria
const desserts = await Recipe.findAll(perspective, {
  where: { 
    status: "recipe://published",
    averageRating: { gt: 4 }
  },
  order: { name: "ASC" },
  limit: 10
});
// Use the fluent query builder
const popularRecipes = await Recipe.query(perspective)
  .where({ averageRating: { gt: 4.5 } })
  .order({ averageRating: "DESC" })
  .limit(5)
  .get();
// Subscribe to real-time updates
await Recipe.query(perspective)
  .where({ status: "recipe://cooking" })
  .subscribe(recipes => {
    console.log("Currently being cooked:", recipes);
  });
// Paginate results
const { results, totalCount, pageNumber } = await Recipe.query(perspective)
  .where({ status: "recipe://published" })
  .paginate(10, 1);

